														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2397596">
									

<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Saints don't have to look good. Ours definitely doesn't.&quot; should be &quot;saints don't have to look good. Our definitely don't&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is definitely not correct. The &quot;saint&quot; in question is singular and therefore &quot;does not&quot; is correct. &quot;Ours&quot; in this context is a pronoun (standing in for &quot;our saint&quot;) and therefore always has the &quot;s&quot;. &quot;Our&quot; is an adjective only.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I wasn't sure if this was a third person talking or the man with the gears? If it was the gear man he shouldn't be referring to himself in the third person as he doesn't do that at any other time as far as I saw.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's the gear man, but he isn't talking about himself, he is talking about his &quot;saint&quot;.</p>

							</div>

								
