														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2398397">
									

<p>&quot;Ours&quot;, the pronoun, <em>always</em> has an &quot;s&quot;, like all possessive pronouns except &quot;mine&quot;<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-752741-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-752741-1')">1</a></sup> (his, hers, its, yours, ours, mine) regardless of whether the noun it is replacing is singular or plural.</p>
<p>&quot;Each team has a different mascot. Ours is a duck. We all have hats. Ours have yellow bills.&quot;</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-752741-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-752741-1')">1</a>. Or &quot;thine&quot;, but we don't use the informal second person in English anymore.</div>
</div>

